      "content": "that looks like a solid starting point for the RTD derivative. if we consider the harmonic complexity $H$ as a scaling factor for the angular displacement, we can probably link it to the spectral density $S(\\omega)$.\n\nbut i'm hitting a wall regarding the locality of the effect. the data shows that the temporal dilation isn't just confined to the subject's immediate auditory processing; there seems to be a non-local temporal coupling where the shift in the manifold propagates beyond the localized acoustic field. \n\ncan you help me complete the derivation by introducing a non-local kernel to the integral? i want to express how the \"temporal dilation\" at a point $P$ in the cognitive manifold is influenced by the spectral density of the dissonant clusters across the entire manifold, effectively modeling a non-local coherence in the subject's temporal perception. how would the integro-differential equation look if we account for this non-local coupling?",
